WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.230
The Climate Fight: What Is the Hard Part?

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.537
There may be more than one road to the same goal,

3
00:00:07.577 --> 00:00:11.400
but the road taken must be neither so strict that states give up

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.910
nor so loose that insufficient effort goes unpunished.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.861
The Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement were

6
00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:20.569
both ratified by many states to slow the warming of

7
00:00:20.609 --> 00:00:23.360
the planet by cutting greenhouse gas emissions.

8
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.160
The Kyoto Protocol sought to back its targets with sanctions;

9
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.733
the states that ratified the Paris Agreement

10
00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:32.800
set their targets freely, without fear of sanctions.

11
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.798
Under Kyoto, only developed countries had

12
00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:38.680
committed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.

13
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.349
The United States, one of the biggest emitters,

14
00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:45.428
did not ratify the protocol, while countries classed as developing,

15
00:00:45.468 --> 00:00:49.500
such as China and India, were exempt from binding reduction targets.

16
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.760
Canada, which had ratified it, became the first state to withdraw.

17
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.861
Because it left before the end of the first period over

18
00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:59.960
which emissions were to be measured, it faced no sanctions.

19
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.320
All the other states with targets met them on paper,

20
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.400
but some got there by buying carbon credits.

21
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.715
And because some major emitters, as developing countries,

22
00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:12.170
were under no obligation to set targets,

23
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.580
the rise in emissions could not be slowed as much as intended.

24
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.861
Under this protocol, the uneven sharing of the

25
00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:20.850
reduction burden and its high cost,

26
00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.380
together with the prospect of sanctions, daunted states.

27
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.492
A state that could not meet its target on its own would

28
00:01:27.532 --> 00:01:30.233
either have to buy carbon credits or be held to a higher

29
00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:33.862
reduction target for the next period: punished, in other words,

30
00:01:33.902 --> 00:01:36.830
with more of the very thing it had already failed to do.

31
00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.135
The Paris Agreement, by contrast,

32
00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:45.590
set out from the start to encourage rather than punish:

33
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.720
that was the price of keeping every party in the game.

34
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.330
So, with no threat of sanctions, what would move states to act?

35
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.212
One might expect a state's effort in this fight to

36
00:01:56.252 --> 00:01:58.501
grow in proportion to the cost of the disasters

37
00:01:58.541 --> 00:02:01.080
that climate change is likely to bring its way.

38
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.267
Yet the overall trend can only be judged fairly if we

39
00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:07.349
bear in mind that not every country fighting emissions

40
00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:10.249
is a developed one, and that states struggling

41
00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:13.040
with more urgent needs are part of this effort too.

42
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.530
Indeed, the Paris Agreement recognized from the outset

43
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.930
that it would take longer for emissions in

44
00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.290
developing countries to stop rising and begin to fall.

45
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.158
The largest sources of the greenhouse gas emissions that

46
00:02:26.198 --> 00:02:29.371
have been climbing since the Industrial Revolution lie in

47
00:02:29.411 --> 00:02:33.040
humanity's basic needs, such as energy production, industry,

48
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.650
transportation, buildings, agriculture and land use.

49
00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.088
Knowing the sources also reveals the

50
00:02:40.128 --> 00:02:42.450
paths to follow in cutting emissions.

51
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.404
But it does not guarantee that every

52
00:02:44.444 --> 00:02:46.480
step to be taken will be equally easy.

53
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.499
In fact, states will pose distinct challenges

54
00:02:49.539 --> 00:02:52.180
because of their differing characteristics.

55
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.597
The more widely an emission source is used,

56
00:02:54.637 --> 00:02:56.210
the harder it is to fight.

57
00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.727
The United Kingdom, for example, came through its tough

58
00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:03.280
test with coal by closing its last coal-fired power plant.

59
00:03:06.545 --> 00:03:09.486
Yet even though coal plants were far fewer than the

60
00:03:09.526 --> 00:03:11.747
vehicles in domestic transportation and the

61
00:03:11.787 --> 00:03:14.760
buildings that need heating, this test took decades.

62
00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.268
With the fight now set to concentrate on

63
00:03:17.308 --> 00:03:20.221
transportation and heating, the test of cutting emissions

64
00:03:20.261 --> 00:03:22.700
has become several notches harder at once.

65
00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.180
The difficulty may also lie in the size of the targets themselves.

66
00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.198
Australia, one of the countries that set an ambitious

67
00:03:30.238 --> 00:03:32.951
reduction target, has made progress with renewables

68
00:03:32.991 --> 00:03:36.881
in its electricity supply; still, it needs wide-ranging

69
00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:40.330
policies and plans to match the scale of its goals.

70
00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.927
But to take effect in time, such plans and policies need both

71
00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:47.600
swift procedures and public support for the changes they bring.

72
00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.882
Being exposed to the dangers of a climate crisis

73
00:03:50.922 --> 00:03:53.428
driven by greenhouse gases will not, by itself,

74
00:03:53.468 --> 00:03:56.430
bring the money and capacity the fight requires.

75
00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.297
The climate crisis, however, needs no visa to cross borders,

76
00:04:00.337 --> 00:04:01.969
and the crises that erupt in

77
00:04:02.009 --> 00:04:05.200
individual countries end up having global effects.

78
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.749
Chile is one of the countries caught

79
00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:11.800
right in the middle of this predicament.

80
00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.633
Add the flood risk forecast along its central coast

81
00:04:14.673 --> 00:04:17.751
to the danger of high temperatures in its northern regions,

82
00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:20.120
and a bigger fight becomes unavoidable;

83
00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.310
Chile does have plans and legislation for this.

84
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.469
But the fight against the climate crisis is also a fight

85
00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:30.040
against its allies, such as a lack of capacity and funding.

86
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:33.759
Even strong economies, to say nothing of developing countries,

87
00:04:33.799 --> 00:04:35.391
find each financial step more

88
00:04:35.431 --> 00:04:38.620
exhausting than the last as they change their habits.

89
00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.906
On the other hand, not every drop in the statistics

90
00:04:41.946 --> 00:04:44.760
is necessarily a sign of success in the fight.

91
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.190
Some statistics mask carbon leakage or the like.

92
00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.217
Japan, for instance, named the decarbonization of

93
00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:53.759
power generation and falling domestic output in

94
00:04:53.799 --> 00:04:57.120
manufacturing as the main reasons its emissions declined.

95
00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:04.259
But a decline in domestic manufacturing does not show that consumption

96
00:05:04.299 --> 00:05:07.770
of goods whose production generates emissions has fallen too.

97
00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.408
In cases like these, then, the emissions do not disappear;

98
00:05:11.448 --> 00:05:14.910
they merely move elsewhere and live on as a global burden.

99
00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.525
Like individuals, states sometimes seem

100
00:05:17.565 --> 00:05:20.120
willing and then back down along the way.

101
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.424
If commitments are backed by sanctions, the loopholes in

102
00:05:23.464 --> 00:05:26.474
agreements must be closed so that no party can walk away

103
00:05:26.514 --> 00:05:29.600
without paying a price, as was possible under Kyoto.

104
00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.141
If they are not, it is probably wiser to build the

105
00:05:36.181 --> 00:05:39.280
main plan around how states have behaved in the past.

106
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.030
Indeed, Canada, the first country to abandon the Kyoto Protocol,

107
00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:44.910
makes the point clearer:

108
00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:47.418
through regulations that halted the fuel charge,

109
00:05:47.458 --> 00:05:50.830
it has done away with mandatory carbon pricing for consumers.

110
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.860
Clearly, the fight against emissions and

111
00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.200
the climate crisis is costly for states.

112
00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.790
Still, they may seem to have stayed in the game.

113
00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.502
So, as governments change and economic conditions

114
00:06:04.542 --> 00:06:07.917
bear down on them, will the lack of sanctions

115
00:06:07.957 --> 00:06:11.090
cause these agreements to turn into group therapy?

116
00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:18.414
We do not know the answer yet, but unless carbon emissions

117
00:06:18.454 --> 00:06:21.880
are cut on a large scale, the world will keep warming.

118
00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:25.814
As storms grow fiercer, we may see a considerable rise

119
00:06:25.854 --> 00:06:29.110
in floods and in the number of species that disappear.

120
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.876
Perhaps, a few decades from now, when scientists look

121
00:06:32.916 --> 00:06:35.640
back at the genetic maps of natural disasters,

122
00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:39.143
they will realize that the disasters yet to come because of the

123
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:41.917
global climate crisis descend from the same lineage

124
00:06:41.957 --> 00:06:45.140
as the ones we have been living through for some time.

